<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; border-radius: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

    <br>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Preserving Our Heritage</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Should We Preserve in the Future?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write personal letters or emails to express opinions. Interactive: Engage in writing exchanges about future aspirations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write emails to peers about the importance of preserving cultural heritage, sharing personal perspectives (e.g., "I have learned about the importance of preserving both architectural sites like Casco Viejo and traditional crafts like mola weaving.") Can participate in writing exchanges discussing the significance of Panama's historical landmarks (e.g., "I believe Portobelo should be preserved because it's part of our history.") Can compose letters to local officials, expressing opinions on cultural preservation efforts (e.g., "It's essential to protect our heritage sites for future generations.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images of Panamanian heritage sites (e.g., Casco Viejo, Panama Canal, Portobelo, a mola, an Emberá village). Students are given 2 minutes to individually write down 2-3 words or short phrases that come to mind for each image, focusing on what they believe is important to preserve. This activates prior knowledge and vocabulary related to "heritage," "sites," and "culture."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to express an opinion and use a relative clause in a short written sentence. For example, the teacher writes on the board: "I believe Casco Viejo, <span style="font-weight: bold;">which</span> is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, should be preserved because it reflects our <span style="font-weight: bold;">history</span>." The teacher points out the relative clause and highlights the target vocabulary. The teacher also briefly models the pronunciation of words like "heritage" (/ˈher.ɪ.tɪdʒ/) and "trade" (/treɪd/).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I write 'Portobelo, <span style="font-weight: bold;">where</span> colonial <span style="font-weight: bold;">trade routes</span> thrived, is a significant <span style="font-weight: bold;">landmark</span>,' am I describing an object or a place?" (A place)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'The Panama Canal <span style="font-weight: bold;">has attracted</span> many <span style="font-weight: bold;">tourists</span> for decades,' does this action happen only once in the past or continue to be relevant now?" (It continues to be relevant now, started in the past)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short email written by a Panamanian student to a friend, discussing the importance of preserving two local heritage sites. The email incorporates relative clauses ("which," "where"), past simple, present perfect, future simple, and relevant vocabulary (e.g., "heritage," "history," "sites," "culture," "preserve," "attract," "legacy," "identity," "efforts"). The email also includes pragmatic functions like "Why is preserving historic landmarks like Casco Viejo or the Panama Viejo ruins important?" and sociolinguistic aspects like discussing heritage respectfully.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Writing: Productive: Write personal letters or emails to express opinions.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the email individually.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main purpose of this email?" (To share opinions about preserving cultural heritage with a peer.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students identify and underline two examples of relative clauses and one example each of past simple, present perfect, and future simple sentences in the email. They also list three vocabulary words from the text related to preservation.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences using relative clauses ("which," "where"), differentiating between past simple and present perfect, and forming future simple predictions. They will incorporate core vocabulary such as "heritage," "history," "sites," "culture," "preserve," "attract," "legacy," "identity," and "efforts."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students complete sentences using "which" or "where," and choose between past simple and present perfect. They also fill in blanks with appropriate vocabulary.
                            <br>Example prompts:
                            <br>a. "Panama Viejo, _____ (which/where) was the first Spanish city on the Pacific coast, _____ (became/has become) a significant historical site."
                            <br>b. "Many <span style="font-weight: bold;">tourists</span> visit the Emberá <span style="font-weight: bold;">villages</span>, _____ (which/where) they _____ (live/have lived) for centuries, to learn about their <span style="font-weight: bold;">traditional</span> <span style="font-weight: bold;">culture</span>."
                            <br>c. "The Panama Canal _____ (opened/has opened) in 1914, and since then, it _____ (attracted/has attracted) <span style="font-weight: bold;">global</span> attention."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Combining):</b> Students combine two simple sentences into one, using "which" or "where," expressing an opinion, and incorporating future simple for predictions.
                            <br>Example prompts:
                            <br>a. "Casco Viejo is an architectural gem. Many <span style="font-weight: bold;">restoration efforts</span> have been made there." → "Casco Viejo, <span style="font-weight: bold;">where</span> many <span style="font-weight: bold;">restoration efforts</span> have been made, is an architectural gem, and I believe these <span style="font-weight: bold;">efforts will continue</span>."
                            <br>b. "Mola <span style="font-weight: bold;">weaving</span> is a traditional craft. It represents the Guna <span style="font-weight: bold;">culture</span>." → "Mola <span style="font-weight: bold;">weaving</span>, <span style="font-weight: bold;">which</span> represents the Guna <span style="font-weight: bold=">culture</span>, is a <span style="font-weight: bold;">relevant</span> <span style="font-weight: bold;">legacy</span>, and I hope we <span style="font-weight: bold;">will preserve</span> it."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students exchange their completed sentences and correct any errors in grammar (relative clauses, tenses) and vocabulary usage. They provide constructive written feedback to their partner.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Written Product – Students will write an email to a peer or a short formal letter to a local official (e.g., from the Ministry of Culture), expressing their opinions on what cultural or historical heritage in Panama should be preserved for future generations and why. They must convince the recipient of the significance of their chosen heritage items/sites.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose 1-2 specific Panamanian heritage elements (e.g., a historical site, a traditional craft, a cultural festival).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They outline their main arguments for preservation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students compose their email/letter (approx. 100-150 words), ensuring they include:
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least two relative clauses (using "which" or "where").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of Past Simple to describe historical facts and Present Perfect to discuss ongoing relevance.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one prediction using Future Simple (e.g., "We <span style="font-weight: bold;">will continue</span> to see its <span style="font-weight: bold;">global</span> <span style="font-weight: bold;">significance</span>.").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A minimum of five target vocabulary words (e.g., "heritage," "landmark," "legacy," "restore," "efforts," "identity").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pragmatic functions of asking about cultural significance and describing historical sites (indirectly through expressing their opinions).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A respectful and interested tone (sociolinguistic aspect).</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to draft, then self-edit for clarity, grammar, and vocabulary before submitting their final version.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation and a writing rubric focusing on content, grammar, vocabulary, and organization.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their completed email or letter from Stage 4. The teacher collects and reviews them.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (on a rubric scale):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the opinion on cultural preservation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate and appropriate use of relative clauses (which/where).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of Past Simple vs. Present Perfect.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of Future Simple for predictions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Integration of target vocabulary words from the theme.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall organization, grammar, and spelling for clarity.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of writing your letter/email about heritage preservation?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which grammatical structure (relative clauses, past simple/present perfect, future simple) did you feel most confident using in your writing today?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well do I feel I expressed my opinion about heritage preservation in writing, ensuring it was clear and convincing?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific vocabulary or grammar point from this lesson will I make an effort to use in my next writing task outside of class?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., Students showed good engagement in expressing opinions; they were able to use relative clauses effectively in combination tasks.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson or future lessons on writing? (e.g., Perhaps more scaffolding for paragraph organization in longer texts; reinforce the difference between 'which' and 'that' for non-defining clauses; provide more varied sentence starters for opinion writing.)</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The opinions and information gathered and expressed in writing during this lesson will serve as a foundation for the Mediation & Integration lesson (Lesson 5), where students will consolidate their understanding and present a 21st Century Skills Project, potentially summarizing their written opinions verbally or collaboratively creating a poster/presentation based on their written arguments.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>